{% aside type="caution" title="Beta" %}
This feature is in beta and may still change.
{% /aside %}

The summary classes can write charts, like the `--plot` option of the CLI, and return the paths of the files they write. PySEGUL can also load the JSON reports of these summaries as Python dictionaries.

## Charts

Pass `PlotOptions` as `plot` to any of these classes:

- [`AlignmentSummary`](/docs/api-usage/python/align_summary/) (not with `per_locus=True`)
- [`AlignmentDistance`](/docs/api-usage/python/align_distance/)
- [`ReadSummary`](/docs/api-usage/python/read_summary/)
- [`ContigSummary`](/docs/api-usage/python/contig_summary/)
- [`MafSummary`](/docs/api-usage/python/maf/)

Charts are written to the `plots` folder inside the output directory.

```python
import pysegul

def summarize_with_charts():
    plot = pysegul.PlotOptions(
        format='svg',
        palette='okabe-ito',
        colormap='cividis',
        )
    summary = pysegul.AlignmentSummary('nexus', 'dna', 'tests/output', plot=plot)
    outputs = summary.from_dir('tests/align-data')
    print(outputs['plots'])
```

All chart options:

| Option | Default | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `format` | `'png'` | `'png'`, `'svg'`, or `'pdf'` |
| `font` | `None` | Font file for PNG and PDF text. Defaults to the system fonts |
| `palette` | `'dark2'` | `'dark2'`, `'okabe-ito'`, `'tol-bright'`, `'tol-muted'`, `'set2'`, `'paired'`, `'set1'` |
| `colormap` | `'viridis'` | `'viridis'`, `'cividis'`, `'magma'`, `'inferno'`, `'plasma'`, `'blues'`, `'greens'`, `'ylgnbu'`, `'ylorrd'`, `'rdbu'`, `'puor'`, `'brbg'` |
| `cvd` | `None` | Simulate a color vision deficiency: `'protanopia'`, `'deuteranopia'`, `'tritanopia'`, `'achromatopsia'` |

PNG and PDF charts need fonts. If none are found, PySEGUL raises a `ValueError`. Set `font` or use `format='svg'`.

### Sample charts

These charts use the default options. The alignment charts come from 4,040 UCE loci of shrews (Esselstyn et al. 2021). The read charts come from raw Illumina reads of *Bunomys* rats.

![Line chart of the number of loci for each minimum share of taxa](../../../../../assets/charts/python/align-completeness.webp)

![Box plots of the share of conserved, variable, parsimony informative, and missing sites across loci](../../../../../assets/charts/python/align-site-proportions.webp)

![Scatter plot of parsimony informative sites against alignment length for each locus](../../../../../assets/charts/python/align-pis-vs-length.webp)

`AlignmentDistance` with `group_by_delimiter('_', 1)` also writes the mean distance between groups:

![Heatmap of mean pairwise distances between groups of samples](../../../../../assets/charts/python/distance-group-mean.webp)

`ReadSummary` in `complete` mode writes charts for all samples and for each read file:

![Line chart of mean quality score by read position for each read file](../../../../../assets/charts/python/read-pos-qscore.webp)

![Line chart of adapter content by read position for one read file](../../../../../assets/charts/python/read-adapter-content.webp)

## Output paths

The `from_dir` and `from_files` methods of the summary classes return a dictionary of output paths. Outputs that were not written are `None`. Every dictionary also has `plots`, a list of chart paths, which is empty without `plot`. Every one of the 21 task classes also exposes a `.report_path` property pointing to its written `report.json` (`<output_prefix>_report.json` when using a prefix).

| Class | Keys |
| --- | --- |
| `AlignmentSummary` | `locus`, `taxon`, `text`, `report` (only `report` written with `per_locus=True` as `None` / empty) |
| `AlignmentDistance` | `summary`, `per_locus_dir`, `group_mean`, `pairwise_long`, `report` |
| `ReadSummary` | `summary`, `positions`, `distributions`, `report` (`positions` and `distributions` are written in `complete` mode only) |
| `ContigSummary` | `summary`, `report` |
| `MafSummary` | `per_file`, `per_species`, `report` |

`MafConversion` returns block and locus counts instead, such as `blocks_read` and `loci_written`; access its report path via `converter.report_path`.

## Reports

Load the full Schema v2 `report.json` envelope (provenance, inputs, parameters, outputs, parent lineage, and statistics) with `load_report(path)`, or load just the parsed summary statistics dictionary with the typed helpers below (which read both v2 `report.json` and legacy v1 summary files):

| Function | Returns |
| --- | --- |
| `load_report(path)` | Full unified v2 `report.json` dictionary |
| `load_align_report(path)` | Alignment summary statistics |
| `load_contig_report(path)` | Contig summary statistics |
| `load_distance_report(path)` | Genetic distances statistics |
| `load_read_report(path, qc_profile=None)` | Read summary statistics and QC checks (`complete` mode) |

```python
import pysegul

def inspect_contig_report():
    summary = pysegul.ContigSummary('auto', 'tests/output')
    outputs = summary.from_dir('tests/contig-data')
    report = pysegul.load_contig_report(outputs['report'])
    envelope = pysegul.load_report(outputs['report'])
    print(report.keys(), envelope['run']['frontend'])
```

The read report also has the read quality checks for each sample. See [Genomic Read Summary](/docs/api-usage/python/read_summary/#quality-checks).

## Reproducibility

Pass `checksum` (`'none'`, `'outputs'`, `'inputs'`, or `'all'`) to any task class constructor—or leave `checksum=None` to inherit the `SEGUL_CHECKSUM` environment variable—to record SHA-256 digests in `report.json`.

| Function | Signature | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `verify_report` | `(path, scope='all', input_dir=None, output_dir=None)` | Verify recorded SHA-256 digests and byte sizes (`scope` is `'all'`, `'inputs'`, or `'outputs'`) |
| `run_report` | `(path, output_dir, input_dir=None, checksum=None, verify_inputs=True)` | Replay any `report.json` (from CLI, SEGUI, or PySEGUL) into `output_dir` |
| `export_workflow` | `(report_paths, output_path, name=None)` | Build a `segul-workflow.json` recipe from chained or ordered report paths |
| `run_workflow` | `(path, output_dir, input_dir=None, checksum=None, verify_inputs=True)` | Replay every step of a `segul-workflow.json` into `output_dir` |
| `export_methods` | `(report_path, format='markdown', include_chain=True, output_path=None)` | Render Methods text (`'markdown'` or `'text'`) from a report, its lineage chain, or a workflow |

```python
import pysegul

summary = pysegul.AlignmentSummary(
    'nexus',
    'dna',
    'tests/output',
    checksum='all',
)
summary.from_dir('tests/align-data')

verification = pysegul.verify_report(summary.report_path, scope='all')
assert verification['passed']

rerun = pysegul.run_report(
    summary.report_path,
    output_dir='tests/output_rerun',
    verify_inputs=True,
)
methods = pysegul.export_methods(summary.report_path, format='markdown')
print(rerun['outputs']['output_dir'])
print(methods)
```

See [Reproducibility and Run Reports](/docs/advance-guides/reproducibility/) for the Schema v2 specification and CLI/GUI interoperability.
